{"id": "066ecefe-219d-46bd-b1b5-fcaf2bb2c2b2", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:36:34.045598", "source": "environment", "tool_name": "terminal", "tool_call_id": "7c5419fe-a034-49f7-9e56-6f87f0e3f1cf", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "open System\nopen Parser\n\n[<EntryPoint>]\nlet main argv =\n    if Array.length argv <> 1 then\n        printfn \"Usage: Calculator <expression>\"\n        1\n    else\n        let expr = argv.[0]\n        let lexbuf = LexBuffer<char>.FromTextReader(new StringReader(expr))\n        try\n            let result = expression Lexer.token lexbuf\n            printfn \"%d\" result\n            0\n        with\n        | ex ->\n            printfn \"Error: %s\" ex.Message\n            1"}], "is_error": false, "command": "cat /Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-b/fsharp-r1/src/Program.fs", "exit_code": 0, "timeout": false, "metadata": {"exit_code": 0, "pid": -1, "username": "ohama", "hostname": "ohama", "working_dir": "/Users/ohama/projs/OpenHandsTests/oh-workdir-planning/arm-b/fsharp-r1", "py_interpreter_path": "", "prefix": "", "suffix": "\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]"}, "full_output_save_dir": "/Users/ohama/.openhands/conversations/478f0b7e8faf473ca01e62a74dd5e283/observations", "kind": "TerminalObservation"}, "action_id": "8ed9ad0f-ffed-43c2-8b65-af1492f5c690", "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "dcfc4fdb-2b0f-436c-9474-10b53c4e19f1", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:36:41.281416", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Now I understand the issue. The `%start` directive in FsYacc does NOT support type annotations like `%start <int> expression`. Looking at the parser grammar:\n\nFrom the parser actions, line 40 shows:\n```\n(fun x -> {x with StartSymbols = x.StartSymbols @ _2})\n```\nwhere `_2` is `'gentype_idents` - just a list of identifiers.\n\nThe type annotation `<int>` on `%start` is NOT supported. The type of the start symbol is inferred from the grammar rules themselves.\n\nSo the fix is to change:\n```\n%start <int> expression\n```\nto:\n```\n%start expression\n```\n\nBut wait, there's another issue. The grammar has a problem:\n```\nexpression:\n  | expr = expr EOF\n      { expr }\n```\n\nThis is a left-recursive rule that references `expr` as the non-terminal name. The `expr` on the RHS is the non-terminal, and `expr` on the LHS is the result variable. This should work, but the naming is confusing.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the grammar, the issue is that `expression` is the start symbol, and it calls `expr`. But `expr` is a left-recursive grammar rule. In FsYacc, left-recursion is handled by precedence declarations.\n\nLet me also check: the grammar uses `expr = expr PLUS e2 = expr` which is left-recursive. With `%left PLUS MINUS` and `%left TIMES DIVIDE`, FsYacc should handle this correctly.\n\nThe main fix needed:\n1. Change `%start <int> expression` to `%start expression`\n\nLet me also verify the grammar is correct. The grammar should be:\n- `expression` is the start symbol, which is `expr EOF`\n- `expr` handles the actual arithmetic with precedence\n\nThis looks correct. Let me fix the `%start` line and rebuild.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "5c2c1708-29ed-4c7c-b39c-42208f6c54a3", "tool_call": {"id": "5c2c1708-29ed-4c7c-b39c-42208f6c54a3", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Now I understand the issue. The `%start` directive in FsYacc does NOT support type annotations like `%start <int> expression`. Looking at the parser grammar:\\n\\nFrom the parser actions, line 40 shows:\\n```\\n(fun x -> {x with StartSymbols = x.StartSymbols @ _2})\\n```\\nwhere `_2` is `'gentype_idents` - just a list of identifiers.\\n\\nThe type annotation `<int>` on `%start` is NOT supported. The type of the start symbol is inferred from the grammar rules themselves.\\n\\nSo the fix is to change:\\n```\\n%start <int> expression\\n```\\nto:\\n```\\n%start expression\\n```\\n\\nBut wait, there's another issue. The grammar has a problem:\\n```\\nexpression:\\n  | expr = expr EOF\\n      { expr }\\n```\\n\\nThis is a left-recursive rule that references `expr` as the non-terminal name. The `expr` on the RHS is the non-terminal, and `expr` on the LHS is the result variable. This should work, but the naming is confusing.\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully at the grammar, the issue is that `expression` is the start symbol, and it calls `expr`. But `expr` is a left-recursive grammar rule. In FsYacc, left-recursion is handled by precedence declarations.\\n\\nLet me also check: the grammar uses `expr = expr PLUS e2 = expr` which is left-recursive. With `%left PLUS MINUS` and `%left TIMES DIVIDE`, FsYacc should handle this correctly.\\n\\nThe main fix needed:\\n1. Change `%start <int> expression` to `%start expression`\\n\\nLet me also verify the grammar is correct. The grammar should be:\\n- `expression` is the start symbol, which is `expr EOF`\\n- `expr` handles the actual arithmetic with precedence\\n\\nThis looks correct. Let me fix the `%start` line and rebuild.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-eb869816-8d94-4eb6-ada5-959b86eb8e94", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Now I understand the issue. The `%start` directive in FsYacc does NOT support type annotations like `%start <int> expression`. Looking at the parser grammar:\\n\\nFrom the parser actions, line 40 shows:\\n```\\n(fun x -> {x with StartSymbols = x.StartSymbols @ _2})\\n```\\nwhere `_2` is `'gentype_idents` - just a list of identifiers.\\n\\nThe type annotation `<int>` on `%start` is NOT supported. The type of the start symbol is inferred from the grammar rules themselves.\\n\\nSo the fix is to change:\\n```\\n%start <int> expression\\n```\\nto:\\n```\\n%start expression\\n```\\n\\nBut wait, there's another issue. The grammar has a problem:\\n```\\nexpression:\\n  | expr = expr EOF\\n      { expr }\\n```\\n\\nThis is a left-recursive rule that references `expr` as the non-terminal name. The `expr` on the RHS is the non-terminal, and `expr` on the LHS is the result variable. This should work, but the naming is confusing.\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully at the grammar, the issue is that `expression` is the start symbol, and it calls `expr`. But `expr` is a left-recursive grammar rule. In FsYacc, left-recursion is handled by precedence declarations.\\n\\nLet me also check: the grammar uses `expr = expr PLUS e2 = expr` which is left-recursive. With `%left PLUS MINUS` and `%left TIMES DIVIDE`, FsYacc should handle this correctly.\\n\\nThe main fix needed:\\n1. Change `%start <int> expression` to `%start expression`\\n\\nLet me also verify the grammar is correct. The grammar should be:\\n- `expression` is the start symbol, which is `expr EOF`\\n- `expr` handles the actual arithmetic with precedence\\n\\nThis looks correct. Let me fix the `%start` line and rebuild.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
