{"id": "c683a550-65a4-4d72-a0e7-f141c6444782", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:40:55.185277", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The FsYacc lexer doesn't seem to have a specific rule for `=`. Looking at the lexer, it has:\n- IDENT (identifier)\n- BAR (`|`)\n- COLON (`:`)\n- And a catch-all that calls `unexpected_char`\n\nThe `=` character is likely falling through to the `unexpected_char` handler. This means FsYacc 11.3.0 doesn't support the `=` syntax for binding names in grammar rules.\n\nLet me check the FsYacc parser grammar to see what syntax is actually supported for binding names. In classic F# FsYacc, the syntax might be different - perhaps using `as` keyword or just positional references.\n\nActually, looking at the lexer more carefully, I see it handles IDENT, BAR, COLON. The `=` is not handled. So I need to use a different syntax.\n\nIn older FsYacc versions, the syntax for binding names in semantic actions might be:\n- Using `as` keyword: `expr as e`\n- Or just using positional references: `_1`, `_2`, etc.\n\nLet me check the parser grammar to understand the expected syntax.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "c8722e2a-0cea-4945-b251-ce8f444d7a50", "tool_call": {"id": "c8722e2a-0cea-4945-b251-ce8f444d7a50", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The FsYacc lexer doesn't seem to have a specific rule for `=`. Looking at the lexer, it has:\\n- IDENT (identifier)\\n- BAR (`|`)\\n- COLON (`:`)\\n- And a catch-all that calls `unexpected_char`\\n\\nThe `=` character is likely falling through to the `unexpected_char` handler. This means FsYacc 11.3.0 doesn't support the `=` syntax for binding names in grammar rules.\\n\\nLet me check the FsYacc parser grammar to see what syntax is actually supported for binding names. In classic F# FsYacc, the syntax might be different - perhaps using `as` keyword or just positional references.\\n\\nActually, looking at the lexer more carefully, I see it handles IDENT, BAR, COLON. The `=` is not handled. So I need to use a different syntax.\\n\\nIn older FsYacc versions, the syntax for binding names in semantic actions might be:\\n- Using `as` keyword: `expr as e`\\n- Or just using positional references: `_1`, `_2`, etc.\\n\\nLet me check the parser grammar to understand the expected syntax.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-964ff822-f6e3-40ee-b339-7a944c9ab3a2", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The FsYacc lexer doesn't seem to have a specific rule for `=`. Looking at the lexer, it has:\\n- IDENT (identifier)\\n- BAR (`|`)\\n- COLON (`:`)\\n- And a catch-all that calls `unexpected_char`\\n\\nThe `=` character is likely falling through to the `unexpected_char` handler. This means FsYacc 11.3.0 doesn't support the `=` syntax for binding names in grammar rules.\\n\\nLet me check the FsYacc parser grammar to see what syntax is actually supported for binding names. In classic F# FsYacc, the syntax might be different - perhaps using `as` keyword or just positional references.\\n\\nActually, looking at the lexer more carefully, I see it handles IDENT, BAR, COLON. The `=` is not handled. So I need to use a different syntax.\\n\\nIn older FsYacc versions, the syntax for binding names in semantic actions might be:\\n- Using `as` keyword: `expr as e`\\n- Or just using positional references: `_1`, `_2`, etc.\\n\\nLet me check the parser grammar to understand the expected syntax.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
