{"id": "37434e48-853b-4452-a78c-f7141e04bab5", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:42:03.799639", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Looking at the AST, I see:\n- `type Rule = Rule of Identifier list * Identifier option * Code option`\n- The `Identifier option` is the precedence symbol (optional)\n\nSo the grammar rule format is: `Rule(symbols, precedence_symbol, code)`\n\nThe symbols list is just a list of identifiers (terminals/non-terminals), not named items. This means the `=` syntax for binding names is NOT supported in this version of FsYacc.\n\nIn classic F# FsYacc, the syntax for binding names in semantic actions uses `as`:\n```\n| e = expr PLUS e2 = expr\n```\nshould be:\n```\n| expr as e PLUS expr as e2\n```\n\nWait, but looking at the lexer, `as` is not a keyword either. Let me check if `as` is treated as an identifier.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the lexer, `as` would be tokenized as an IDENT. So the syntax might be:\n```\n| expr as e PLUS expr as e2\n```\n\nBut wait, the lexer only recognizes IDENT, BAR, COLON, and a few other things. The `as` keyword would be tokenized as an IDENT.\n\nLet me look at the parser grammar more carefully to understand the expected syntax for production items.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "f68615a2-5b5c-4023-b8a6-570617e97f33", "tool_call": {"id": "f68615a2-5b5c-4023-b8a6-570617e97f33", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Looking at the AST, I see:\\n- `type Rule = Rule of Identifier list * Identifier option * Code option`\\n- The `Identifier option` is the precedence symbol (optional)\\n\\nSo the grammar rule format is: `Rule(symbols, precedence_symbol, code)`\\n\\nThe symbols list is just a list of identifiers (terminals/non-terminals), not named items. This means the `=` syntax for binding names is NOT supported in this version of FsYacc.\\n